{"records":[{"id":"vy2cbjn3my","title":"Raw images","description":"Raw  images  of \"Ubiquitination-directed cytosolic DNA degradation drives cGAS-STING-mediated immune response upon DNA damage\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-09T11:52:12.037Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vy2cbjn3my","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vy2cbjn3my"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lei","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Ionizing Radiation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j3p84sb38k","title":"MDC1 maintains active elongation complexes of RNA polymerase II","description":"Current biochemical data indicate a functional interplay of MDC1 DNA damage response scaffold protein with the transcription and splicing machinery both in unchallenged conditions and upon DNA damage. The PDF file includes wider areas of scanned western blots which are already included in the main manuscript entitled ''MDC1 maintains active elongation complexes of RNA polymerase II''. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-01T17:28:50.485Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j3p84sb38k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j3p84sb38k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Georgios ","last_name":"Pappas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jfhwwhczx8","title":"PP2A controls genome integrity by integrating nutrient sensing and metabolic pathways with the DNA damage response_Ferrari et al.2017","description":"Synthetic genetic array (SGA), Metabolomics and Unprocessed data represented in the paper \"PP2A controls genome integrity by integrating nutrient sensing and metabolic pathways with the DNA damage response\" by Ferrari et al. 2017.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-05-31T08:38:59.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jfhwwhczx8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jfhwwhczx8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elisa","last_name":"Ferrari","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Oligonucleotide","Western Blot","Metabolism","Image Analysis (Medical Imaging)","Sensitivity Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mk7z7st438","title":"ATR restrains DNA synthesis and mitotic catastrophe in response to CDC7 inhibition. Rainey et al","description":"Background:\nCDC7 kinase is required for activation of licensed origins of replication during the process of DNA duplication. CDC7 kinase inhibitors interfere with this process, limiting both DNA synthesis and cell proliferation.\n\nAim:\nIdentify genes responsible for limiting cell proliferation in response to inhibition of CDC7 using small molecule ATP-competitive inhibitors.\n\nStrategy:\nA CRISPR/Cas9 loss of function screen was performed to identify genes that upon loss allow bypass of a proliferation blockade imposed by the CDC7 inhibitor, XL413.\n\nData:\nRepresents source data for main figures and supplemental items in support of the Manuscript 'ATR restrains DNA synthesis and mitotic catastrophe in response to CDC7 inhibition', Rainey et al.\n\nData collection, interpretation and findings are addressed in the manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-19T20:26:38.851Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mk7z7st438","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mk7z7st438"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Rainey","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","DNA Replication","Cell Signaling","Genetic Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j4kdb59xyf","title":"BRD4 Prevents R-Loop Formation and Transcription-Replication Conflicts by Ensuring Efficient Transcription Elongation","description":"Effective spatio-temporal control of transcription and replication during S-phase is paramount to maintaining genomic integrity and cell survival. Dysregulation of these systems can lead to conflicts between the transcription and replication machinery causing DNA damage and cell death. BRD4, a BET bromodomain protein and known transcriptional regulator, interacts with P-TEFb to ensure efficient transcriptional elongation by stimulating phosphorylation of RNA Polymerase II (RNAPII). Here we report that disruption of BET bromodomain protein function causes RNAPII pausing on the chromatin and DNA damage affecting cells in S-phase. We find that this persistent, RNAPII-dependent pausing leads to accumulation of RNA:DNA hybrids (R-loops), which are known to lead to transcription-replication conflicts (TRCs), DNA damage, and cell death. Furthermore, we show that resolution of R-loops abrogates BET bromodomain inhibitor-induced DNA damage, and that BET bromodomain inhibition induces both R-loops and DNA damage at sites of BRD4 occupancy. Finally, we see that the BRD4 C-terminal domain, which interacts with P-TEFb, is required to prevent R-loop formation and DNA damage caused by BET bromodomain inhibition and that oncogenes which promote transcription and replication exacerbate BET bromodomain inhibitor-induced DNA damage. Together, these findings demonstrate that BET bromodomain inhibitors can damage DNA via induction of R-loops and TRCs in highly replicative cells. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-14T04:22:12.257Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j4kdb59xyf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j4kdb59xyf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Drake","last_name":"Edwards","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wpgk3sbskj","title":"Sch9S6K controls DNA repair and DNA damage response efficiency in aging cells. Lucca et al.","description":"Abstract \nSurvival from UV-induced DNA lesions relies on nucleotide excision repair (NER) and the Mec1ATR DNA damage response (DDR). We study DDR and NER in aging cells and find that old cells struggle to repair DNA and\nactivate Mec1ATR. We employ pharmacological and genetic approaches to rescue DDR and NER during aging. Conditions activating Snf1AMPK rescue DDR functionality, but not NER, while inhibition of the TORC1-\nSch9S6K axis restores NER and enhances DDR by tuning PP2A activity, specifically in aging cells. Age-related\nrepair deficiency depends on Snf1AMPK-mediated phosphorylation of Sch9S6K on Ser160 and Ser163. PP2A\nactivity in old cells is detrimental for DDR and influences NER by modulating Snf1AMPK and Sch9S6K. Hence,\nthe DDR and repair pathways in aging cells are influenced by the metabolic tuning of opposing AMPK and\nTORC1 networks and by PP2A activity. Specific Sch9S6K phospho-isoforms control DDR and NER efficiency,\nspecifically during aging.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-31T08:01:33.723Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wpgk3sbskj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wpgk3sbskj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elisa","last_name":"Ferrari","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Repair","Protein Phosphatase","Aging","Saccharomyces cerevisiae","Ribosomal Protein S6 Kinase Beta-1"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"twy2gg3kgd","title":"Suppression of TREX1 Deficiency-Induced Cellular Senescence and Interferonopathies by Inhibition of DNA Damage Response","description":"Uncropped images from western blots","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:14:03.871Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/twy2gg3kgd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/twy2gg3kgd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qi","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Innate Immune System"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k4fmj3nzfz","title":"Ubiquitin Phosphorylation at Thr12 Modulates the DNA Damage Response. Walser et al","description":"Modification of ubiquitin by phosphorylation is a new and largely unexplored concept in the field. Here we found that ubiquitin phosphorylation at Thr12 (pUbT12) represents a novel histone mark, H2AK15pUbT12, which regulates the DNA damage response by inhibiting 53BP1-mediated DNA repair. \nDetectable as chromatin modification on H2AK15ub, pUbT12 accumulates in nuclear foci and is increased upon DNA damage. Mutating Thr12 prevents the removal of ubiquitin from H2AK15ub by USP51 deubiquitinating enzyme, leading to a pronounced accumulation of ubiquitinated chromatin. Chromatin modified by pUbT12 is inaccessible to 53BP1, but permissive to the homologous recombination (HR) proteins RNF169, RAD51 and BRCA1/BARD1 complex. Phosphorylation of ubiquitin at Thr12 in the chromatin context is a new histone mark – H2AK15pUbT12 – that regulates the DDR by hampering the activity of 53BP1 at damaged chromosomes.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-06T21:25:15.558Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k4fmj3nzfz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k4fmj3nzfz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lorenza","last_name":"Penengo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Fluorescence Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vth6c4x9pp","title":"A single RNA polymerase II ubiquitylation site coordinates transcription with the DNA damage response","description":"In response to transcription-blocking DNA damage, cells orchestrate a multi-pronged\nreaction, involving transcription-coupled DNA repair, degradation of RNAPII and\ngenome-wide transcription shutdown. How these responses are connected has\nremained unclear. Here we show that damage-induced ubiquitylation of RNAPII itself,\nat a single lysine (RPB1 K1268), is the focal point for DNA damage response\ncoordination. K1268-ubiquitylation affects DNA repair and signals RNAPII degradation,\nessential for surviving genotoxic insult. It is also crucial for transcriptional shutdown,\nin the absence of which cells display dramatic transcriptome alterations. Additionally,\nregulation of RNAPII stability is central to transcription recovery – indeed, depletion of\nthe RNAPII pool underlies the failure of this process in Cockayne syndrome B cells.\nThese data expose regulation of global RNAPII levels as integral to the cellular DNA\ndamage response, and open the intriguing possibility that RNAPII pool size generally\naffects cell-specific transcription programmes, in genome instability disorders and\neven normal cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-20T16:44:11.686Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vth6c4x9pp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vth6c4x9pp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ana","last_name":"Tufegdzic Vidakovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vesela","last_name":"Encheva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bram","last_name":"Snijders","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jesper","last_name":"Svejstrup","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Expression Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122058,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":129,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122058,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121605,"restricted":60,"embargoed":393},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2048},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1381},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":559},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":417},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":344},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":299},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":245}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119495},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1890},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":174},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":126},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":70},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":55},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":38},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d